    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-2-271-2002",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The availability of near-real time ozone observations from satellite instruments has recently initiated the development of ozone data assimilation systems. In this paper we present the results of an ozone assimilation and forecasting system, in use since Autumn 2000. The forecasts are produced by an ozone transport and chemistry model, driven by the operational medium range forecasts of ECMWF. The forecasts are initialised with realistic ozone distributions, obtained by the assimilation of near-real time total column observations of the GOME spectrometer on ERS-2. The forecast error diagnostics demonstrate that the system produces meaningful total ozone forecasts for up to 6 days in the extratropics. In the tropics meaningful forecasts of the small anomalies are restricted to shorter periods of about two days with the present model setup. It is demonstrated that important events, such as the breakup of the South Pole ozone hole and mini-hole events above Europe can be successfully predicted 4--5 days in advance.",
    "field": "Ozone forecasting and data assimilation",
    "problem": "Producing accurate, medium-range global ozone forecasts is challenging because the quality depends on effectively initializing atmospheric chemistry models with realistic and timely ozone distributions, a task that has only recently become feasible with the availability of near-real-time satellite observations.",
    "method": "The paper presents a global ozone forecasting system where a transport-chemistry model, driven by ECMWF meteorological forecasts, is initialized by assimilating near-real-time total column ozone observations from the GOME instrument on the ERS-2 satellite.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and evaluate a global ozone forecasting system that assimilates near-real-time satellite ozone observations from the GOME instrument into a chemistry-transport model to produce medium-range forecasts.",
